            <ArticleTitle>Caesarean delivery and risk of developing asthma in the offspring.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>595-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the association between caesarean section and risk of developing asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">We evaluated this association in a Danish cohort, comprising of 11,147 mothers and their babies of which 7119 mother-child pairs were included in the analyses. The mothers' reported asthma data on their children were linked to hospitalization records on mode of delivery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The adjusted odds ratio for developing asthma was 1.11 (95% CI, 0.88-1.39) for caesarean sections versus vaginal births.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We found no evidence that children being delivered by caesarean section have an increased risk of asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
